Install the packages, then run one file: `npm install express express-session bcryptjs`. `bcryptjs` is pure JavaScript, so it needs no native build step.

**What this does:** It adds a login page, a logout button, and a `/dashboard` page that only logged-in users can see. Passwords are stored as bcrypt hashes, never as plain text. The login state lives in a server-side session tied to a cookie. This demo keeps users in memory, so the demo user is lost on restart. Nothing here modifies a database or other files.

### How to use it

1. In your project folder, run `npm install express express-session bcryptjs`.
2. Save the code below as `app.js` in that folder.
3. Start the app with a session secret: `SESSION_SECRET=$(openssl rand -hex 32) node app.js` (on Windows PowerShell, set `$env:SESSION_SECRET` first, then run `node app.js`).
4. Open `http://localhost:3000`, then log in with `alice` / `secret123`.

### The script

```js
// app.js
const express = require('express');
const session = require('express-session');
const bcrypt = require('bcryptjs');

const app = express();

// Read form posts (username and password fields) into req.body
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: false }));

// Sessions: the cookie holds only an ID; the user data stays on the server.
app.use(session({
  secret: process.env.SESSION_SECRET || 'dev-only-change-me', // always set this in real deployments
  resave: false,
  saveUninitialized: false,           // don't create a session until someone logs in
  cookie: {
    httpOnly: true,                   // JavaScript in the browser can't read the cookie
    sameSite: 'lax',                  // limits cross-site request forgery
    secure: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production', // HTTPS-only cookie in production
    maxAge: 1000 * 60 * 60 * 2        // log out after 2 hours
  }
}));

// Demo user store. Replace with a database table in real use.
// The password is stored only as a bcrypt hash.
const users = [
  { username: 'alice', passwordHash: bcrypt.hashSync('secret123', 10) }
];

// Middleware: lets the request continue only if someone is logged in
function requireLogin(req, res, next) {
  if (req.session.user) return next();
  res.redirect('/login');
}

// Show the login form
app.get('/login', (req, res) => {
  res.send(`
    <h2>Log in</h2>
    <form method="post" action="/login">
      <input name="username" placeholder="Username" required><br>
      <input name="password" type="password" placeholder="Password" required><br>
      <button>Log in</button>
    </form>`);
});

// Check the credentials
app.post('/login', async (req, res) => {
  const { username, password } = req.body;
  const user = users.find(u => u.username === username);
  const ok = user && await bcrypt.compare(password || '', user.passwordHash);

  if (!ok) {
    return res.status(401).send('Wrong username or password. <a href="/login">Try again</a>');
  }

  // Create a fresh session ID on login (prevents session fixation attacks)
  req.session.regenerate(err => {
    if (err) return res.status(500).send('Login failed. Please try again.');
    req.session.user = { username: user.username };
    res.redirect('/dashboard');
  });
});

// Log out: destroy the session on the server
app.post('/logout', (req, res) => {
  req.session.destroy(() => res.redirect('/login'));
});

// Protected page: requireLogin runs first
app.get('/dashboard', requireLogin, (req, res) => {
  res.send(`
    <h2>Welcome, ${req.session.user.username}</h2>
    <form method="post" action="/logout"><button>Log out</button></form>`);
});

app.get('/', (req, res) => res.redirect('/dashboard'));

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Running at http://localhost:3000'));
```

### Customization tips

- **Real users:** swap the `users` array for a database query. Keep `bcrypt.hash` at signup and `bcrypt.compare` at login.
- **Session storage:** the default in-memory store loses sessions on restart and doesn't work across multiple server instances. For production, use a store such as `connect-pg-simple` (PostgreSQL) or `connect-redis`.
- **Production checklist:** serve over HTTPS, set `NODE_ENV=production`, add rate limiting to `/login`, and add password reset.
- **Ready-made option:** if you need sign-up flows, email verification, MFA, or SSO, a provider such as Auth0 or Clerk is usually less work than building those yourself.

**Time estimate:** about 15 minutes to get this demo running. Swapping in a real user table with signup and password reset is an afternoon.

Next: run `node app.js`, open `http://localhost:3000/dashboard` in a private window, and confirm it redirects you to `/login`.
